PRACTICAL 4: HOW DEVOPS PRINCIPLES (CI/CD) IMPROVE DEPLOYMENT AND
UPDATES FOR MACHINE LEARNING PROJECTS
==================================================================
 
1. WHAT IS DEVOPS?
DevOps is a set of practices that joins development (writing code) and
operations (deploying and running it) through automation, shared
ownership and fast feedback. Applied to machine learning it is called
MLOps.
 
2. WHY ML PROJECTS NEED IT
An ML project has three things that change: code, data and the model.
Without automation, teams face "works on my machine" problems, models
that cannot be reproduced, manual and error-prone deployment, and
silent drops in model accuracy.
 
3. CORE DEVOPS PRINCIPLES
- Automation: build, test and deploy without manual steps.
- Version control: code, configs and data versions are all tracked
  (Git, DVC).
- Continuous feedback: every change is validated within minutes.
- Collaboration: one shared pipeline for data scientists and engineers.
- Infrastructure as Code: environments are defined in files
  (Dockerfile, requirements.txt, YAML).
- Monitoring: production models are watched for errors and drift.
 
4. CONTINUOUS INTEGRATION (CI)
Every push to GitHub automatically triggers:
- code checkout
- dependency installation in a clean environment
- linting and code style checks
- unit tests (data validation, feature functions, model functions)
- a quick training or smoke run to confirm the pipeline still works
Benefit: bugs and broken code are caught early, and the main branch
stays stable.
 
5. CONTINUOUS DELIVERY / DEPLOYMENT (CD)
After CI passes, the pipeline automatically:
- trains or retrains the model
- evaluates it against a minimum accuracy threshold
- packages it (pickle/joblib file or Docker image)
- stores it in a model registry or artifact store
- deploys it to staging, then production (API, batch job, cloud)
Benefit: updates are released quickly, repeatably and with a rollback
option.
 
6. CONTINUOUS TRAINING (CT) - SPECIFIC TO ML
The pipeline retrains and redeploys the model automatically when new
data arrives or when monitoring detects performance decay.
 
7. TYPICAL ML CI/CD PIPELINE
Code push -> Checkout -> Install dependencies -> Lint -> Unit tests ->
Train model -> Evaluate -> Package/Archive model -> Deploy -> Monitor
 
8. BENEFITS FOR ML PROJECTS
- Faster releases: model updates reach users in minutes, not weeks.
- Reproducibility: the same code and environment give the same result.
- Higher quality: automated tests and accuracy checks block bad models.
- Lower risk: small frequent changes are easier to debug and roll back.
- Better teamwork: everyone works on a shared, tested codebase.
- Traceability: every model is linked to a commit, build and dataset.
 
9. COMMON TOOLS
- Version control: Git, GitHub, DVC
- CI/CD: Jenkins, GitHub Actions, GitLab CI
- Containers: Docker, Kubernetes
- Experiment tracking and registry: MLflow, Weights & Biases
- Testing: pytest, Great Expectations
- Monitoring: Prometheus, Grafana, Evidently
 
10. CHALLENGES
- Large datasets and models are hard to version with plain Git.
- Training can be slow and need GPUs, so pipelines must be designed
  carefully.
- ML results are probabilistic, so tests use accuracy thresholds
  instead of exact outputs.
- Data drift can degrade a model after deployment.
 
11. CONCLUSION
CI/CD brings automation, consistency and fast feedback to the ML
lifecycle, which makes deployments and model updates faster, safer and
reproducible.
 
 
